# Expression waves — source-to-UI map (CHG-0052)

Source inspection order: `design/references/background.png`, then `background_text.png`, both at 941 × 32,127. The clean background omits the luminous ornament. The composite includes it, but also bakes in English heading, subtitle, button and UNO wordmark. The page must keep those words and the button as live localized HTML.

| Layer ID | Reference extent in 941px source | Live target | Extraction constraint |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| EW-L left rising arc and terminal light | x≈130–360, y≈14670–14880 | decorative image behind `#expressions`, subtitle and B08 | Exclude all heading pixels from the composite |
| EW-R right descending arc and terminal light | x≈650–810, y≈14820–15050 | same decorative image | Exclude the baked B08 surface and label |
| EW-G four short horizontal glints | x≈380–565, y≈14702, 14856, 14916, 15045 | same decorative image | Keep only the glints, not text/button |

Extract the RGB pixels of these masked regions from the composite over the clean image, with transparent pixels elsewhere. Do not redraw the waves or serve the composite as a background. Place the derived `public/art/expression-waves.png` at source x=120, y=14665, width=700 (source CSS scale 402/941 on Pro); keep it noninteractive and hidden from assistive technology. The existing live `expressions` and `choose` text boxes center on source x=470.5. B08 retains its mapped reference center at x=465.5 (about 2 CSS px left of the text axis on Pro), with localized pill widths provided by `lib/button-localized-widths.json`; this small reference offset should not be “corrected” to an arbitrary new position. Verify on real Pro/Pro Max WebKit screenshots against the source crop and check all 11 locales for centered text/button, overflow, visible waves, and clickable B08.

## Applied and checked

`tools/generate-expression-waves.mjs` produces the 700×400 transparent PNG; `components/Story.tsx` places it below live content and lazy-loads it. Real Pro/Pro Max WebKit screenshots, a contact sheet covering all 11 locales, the aligned Pro diff, behavior log and lazy/performance checks are under [expression-wave evidence](evidence/expression-waves/). The 5-source-px B08 center offset is present in the reference.
     1	# Ramsider UNO — project rules
     2
     3	Build the new product site from `design/references/`. **Design and implement for iPhone 17 Pro first, then verify iPhone 17 Pro Max; adapt responsively to every other screen size.** The real mobile page must match the supplied reference while loading quickly and working accessibly. One user request should produce one complete delivery: perform as many internal inspect → implement → verify → fix cycles as needed, then code review, performance work, and final re-verification. Visual slices are internal work units; finish and integrate the whole requested page or feature before delivery. Do not hand the user a first draft to find ordinary defects. “Done” means every applicable gate has passed with evidence, not that code compiles or that zero bugs have been assumed.
     4
     5	These are the mandatory rules. [docs/discipline.md](docs/discipline.md) defines stage gates and failure routing; [CONTEXT_INDEX.md](CONTEXT_INDEX.md) routes to other details. For substantive project work, read the short [MEMORY.md](MEMORY.md) for current state, then only the topic files relevant to the task. `PROMPT.txt` and `SUMMARY.txt` are source records for unresolved questions; current user instructions and current assets take precedence over their examples or suggestions.
     6
     7	## Mandatory change and defect log
     8
     9	[CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md) is the authoritative record of **every logical project change and every discovered defect**, including code, assets, content, mappings, tests, documentation, configuration, user corrections and agent-discovered regressions. The agent maintains it **without a user reminder or a separate permission request**: create/update the entry at discovery, record the solution and changed files when applied, then append verification and approval before delivery. Search relevant IDs when resuming work; do not wait until the final answer to reconstruct the history.
    10
    11	Use the journal's required fields and lifecycle. Record what was wrong/missing/different, expected result, reference/request, cause, solution, intermediate mapping/specification and how it was applied, changed paths, checks and evidence. Every claimed fix needs a dated **technical approval**, naming the verifier and its evidence (and independent auditor when applicable). `APPLIED_UNVERIFIED` is not `VERIFIED`; blocked/skipped checks cannot supply approval. Explicit user approval is a separate field: never invent it or require it for routine authorized fixes. Reopen a failed fix under the same ID and preserve the previous verdict and failure history. Track remaining defects as open entries, even when outside the current task.
    12
    13	For every defect, explain the agent's observable mistaken decision/action, the missed check, responsibility with evidence, and a concrete behavior change. Distinguish genuinely changed/missing inputs from agent failures; do not blame the user merely because an input was absent. Unknown causes stay explicit. Link repeatable causes to LESSONS and an Agent Eval case with its actual run status; a fixed UI does not prove improved agent behavior. Before similar work or a fresh rebuild, retrieve those lessons and turn prevention into acceptance checks. Repository memory improves instructions/context, not model weights.
    14
    15	Small edits may use a short entry, but cannot omit logging or proportionate verification. Group related edits under one logical ID; do not log every tool call. A delivery is incomplete if its journal records or required technical approval are missing. MEMORY describes current state; LESSONS explains reusable prevention; neither replaces CHANGELOG.
    16
    17	## Work in stages
    18
    19	1. **Understand before coding.** Check repository state, [MEMORY.md](MEMORY.md), relevant lessons, and the requested scope. Inspect `design/references/background.png` first, then `background_text.png`, then relevant fonts, button artwork, and source layers. Crop the 941 × 32,127 reference into legible sections. Map content, controls, art layers, section boundaries, and the source-to-CSS viewport scale; set observable acceptance checks and identify unverified product facts. Use [DESIGN.md](DESIGN.md) and [PRODUCT.md](PRODUCT.md). **Exit:** the target slice, assets, behavior, and comparison method are understood; unresolved inputs are explicit.
    20	2. **Implement one visual slice.** Build for the Pro baseline first, using the clean background as artwork and semantic HTML/CSS for text and controls. Keep text translatable and controls usable; no inert CTA or invented checkout. Reproduce reference copy in the local build while tracking unverified factual claims for publication. Use Next.js + React + strict TypeScript with the framework's own build toolchain; choose each route's rendering mode and Server/Client boundaries from its data and interaction needs. Do not revive the deleted app. Use [ARCHITECTURE.md](ARCHITECTURE.md). **Exit:** the slice renders and its required behavior works; continue through all slices in the requested scope.
    21	3. **Verify the actual pixels.** Open the running page in a real browser at the iPhone 17 Pro viewport, then verify Pro Max. Confirm the page's actual CSS layout width matches each test profile (the viewport meta tag must be correct). Wait for fonts/images and capture screenshots. Compare the Pro image to the calibrated reference crop; inspect Pro Max as a responsive target unless it has its own reference. The verifier must see screenshots, not only DOM/CSS measurements. Fix material differences and capture again. Use a tolerant pixel diff as a second signal when aligned. Use [docs/visual-qa.md](docs/visual-qa.md). **Exit:** no material mismatch remains in the supported target composition; otherwise keep iterating or report a concrete external blocker without saying “done.”
    22	4. **Check behavior and adaptation.** Test relevant clicks, hover, scroll, navigation, forms, menus, animation states, keyboard access, Arabic RTL, mobile safe areas, and representative tablet/desktop widths. Check overflow, clipping, crops, wraps, spacing, and tile seams. Use [docs/mobile.md](docs/mobile.md). **Exit:** required states work at the sizes they support.
    23	5. **Review and optimize.** For code changes, run Codex's native `/review` on the actual diff before delivery (CLI equivalent when needed), examine every actionable finding, fix it, and rerun affected checks. Run available type, lint, test, and production-build checks. Review component boundaries, duplication, accessibility, and unused code. Measure first render, LCP/INP/CLS where measurable, network bytes, JavaScript, layout shifts, and scroll smoothness in a production build. Reassess static, request-time server, and client rendering against measured latency, freshness, and client cost; change strategy where it improves the result. Fix meaningful problems, then repeat affected visual/browser checks. Use [QUALITY.md](QUALITY.md) and [PERFORMANCE.md](PERFORMANCE.md). **Exit:** no known material code, visual, accessibility, or speed regression remains; report if `/review` was unavailable rather than counting a manual pass as its result.
    24	6. **Learn and report evidence.** Complete the affected [CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md) entries with actual checks, technical approval and remaining open IDs. Refresh [MEMORY.md](MEMORY.md) when verified state, decisions, or blockers changed; record repeatable errors in [LESSONS.md](LESSONS.md). Use [docs/definition-of-done.md](docs/definition-of-done.md). State tested viewports, screenshot comparisons, interactions, build checks, performance measurements, remaining differences, and checks that could not run. Never claim pixel fidelity, WCAG compliance, a 95+ Lighthouse score, or completion without the corresponding evidence.
    25
    26	**Do not mark a failed gate passed or delegate ordinary QA to the user.** Route the failure back to the stage that can fix it, repeat that stage, and rerun downstream checks the fix could affect. A blocked external dependency must not stop independent work. For UI work, the implement → browser screenshot → compare → fix → recheck loop is mandatory after each completed visual slice and after later appearance changes; repeat it on the assembled page. Work autonomously through all feasible checks; resolve routine choices from the repository and references, and seek only irreducible external facts or access while continuing independent work. For a small non-UI task, apply only relevant stages.
    27
    28	For substantive project work, start one independent, non-editing **discipline auditor** alongside scope inspection. Reuse it at each applicable stage gate: scope before implementation; each completed UI slice's rendering and visual evidence; behavior/adaptation; code review and performance; and final integration, memory, and delivery evidence. Give it acceptance checks, changed files, browser images, command results, and any other agents' findings as relevant. Wait for its evidence-based pass/fail/blocked finding before closing a gate. Fix failures and re-audit affected gates; the main agent owns implementation and required checks. Use the project [auditor profile](.codex/agents/discipline_auditor.toml) when available and [supervision protocol](docs/supervision.md); if the profile cannot be selected, give a subagent the same non-editing assignment. Do not spawn it for a trivial edit. The auditor supplements real QA and native `/review`; it cannot certify missing evidence.
    29
    30	## Standing constraints

